            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we had identified a few locations where excess air leakage &amp; heat loss occurs:



** The front bedroom window does not shut/seal well and requires an adjustment 



** The header space in the basement, where it is open &amp; visible in the laundry &amp; furnace rooms 



** Around the furnace &amp; AC pipes through the side basement wall



** The range hood fan (although it vents up &amp; out) is not sealed well &amp; I had you feel the large amount of air leakage from behind



** The attic hatch does not seal well &amp; can be sealed with a removable caulking such as Draft Attack



** Wood trim above the basement door



Savings: A modest 10% reduction in air leakage is estimated to save $ 72 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.1 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 0.82 energy factor (EF) Rinnai hot instantaneous hot water heater ......... that you had indicated has been giving you some problems. Should you consider replacing this unit, please choose an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an EF of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an EF of 0.92, could potentially save $ 46 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.6 GJ and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house has a high efficiency (95% AFUE) furnace as the primary heating system. The "Header" at the beginning of this section indicates that I am recommending you replace this with another 95% AFUE furnace ......... I am not - the wording above is just a bug in the software.



In addition, there are 2 other supplemental heaters:



1. An older Williams space heater in the basement that you indicated is used on a regular basis throughout the winter, that draws its' combustion air from inside the house (ie: not direct vent) and has a continuous pilot light. A newer Williams heater of the same design would have a rated efficiency of 68%, and although yours is likely lower, I used 68% for my analysis. I have assumed 50% use of this heater for the family room space it occupies. As the thermostat for the primary furnace is upstairs, it is reasonable to say the basement would be cooler without the use of the Williams space heater.



2. A 3-sided fireplace is installed on the main floor, is direct vent with spark ignition (ie: no pilot light) and is only used intermittently and not considered as "required" for the heating of your home.



Although the current Williams heater is a design that can operate when the power is out and some of its' features result in the lower efficiency, my focus is to look for ways to enhance the overall heating efficiency. I have seen direct-vent propane space heaters (I think Rinnai has one) that are direct vent, have a spark ignition, and have rated efficiencies of about 81%.



I modeled an upgrade from the Williams space heater at 68% efficiency (not direct-vent &amp; has a continuous pilot) to one with 81% efficiency (direct-vent &amp; spark ignition) to be used 50% of the time to supplement the heating of the basement family room



Savings: The above mentioned upgrade could potentially save you $ 339 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 14.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates for space heaters.

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting (your house has mostly these older inefficient lights), and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Flourescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 5.6 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace &amp; main floor fireplace are "direct vent" equipment that is not susceptible to back-drafting, however the older Williams heater in the basement is not "direct vent" and could have combustion gases back-drafted into the house.



During my assessment of your home, I do recall seeing smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors, however some were missing and others appeared older than 10 years. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood

        - Two bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer



If you are considering upgrading bathroom exhaust fans, please choose ENERGY STAR rated units that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.38 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.03 per litre 

Propane - $2.46 per m3 for the piped system; or $0.62 per litre delivered 

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.09 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
